    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/1/81019.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 1,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, so I don't need to get people back on the coronavirus. It is now spreading in our country that we have the ability to contain the infection. It's particularly a problem for the older person or the person with an underlying condition, heart disease or lung problems or such like that. So it's important that we contain it as soon as we can. So I've been working with the administration on several ideas. One idea that I've had is we've got to make sure that medications are affordable for the senior citizen who might need them. If she or he is on the Medicare Part D plan, And under the current benefit, under the current arrangement, if they hit their catastrophic portion and those senior citizens know what I'm speaking about, they have to pay 5% of the list price no matter how high that list price is. I'm trying to get legislation through that would trap the amount the senior would have to pay. So even if it ends up costing a million dollars for the medicine, the senior citizen would be able to afford it because the amount they're obligated to pay is trapped. So that's one thing we're doing. The second thing we're doing as, you know, kind of as part of a bigger effort, how do we protect that family from a surprise medical bill? It can happen anytime, but there's going to be more people going to the emergency room to be checked out. And if they find out they go to an in-network hospital to get billed for an out-of-network doctor, They may have a $4,000 bill when all they wanted to do is get a blood test for coronavirus. So we're also working on legislation that would protect families from surprise medical billing, because other things we're doing in particular for coronavirus, but I think these two are the most important that we can get through as soon as possible.",
