    "episode_id": 81019,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/1/81019.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 6,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, the most expensive year of a child's life is the first year. Think about those diapers, the crib, the clothing you have to buy, and it's really a strain for a young family. If somebody has lower income, they may have quick work. If they want to stay at home in breastfeed, if they quick work, then obviously there's a higher likelihood they go on government assistance. So cost society, when somebody does quick work, they're not paying taxes, et cetera, But it also costs society when somebody goes back to work and she can't breastfeed or she can't take care of her own child should she wish to because we know that if a mother and baby bond, then that child is going to do better and the mama does better. And then society does better. What we're doing, we're taking advantage of the fact that Republicans just increased the child tax credit from $1,000 to $2,000 a year. And what we allow is for the day of birth or the day of adoption for the parents to get a $5,000 lump sum. And we allow them to do this by pulling forward that child tax credit they received over the next 10 years. Instead of $2,000, they'll get 1,500 for the next 10 years until everything is paid back. So we think it's a great idea. It should not increase the deficit. It does allow the parent at their option, no mandate, no mandate, they don't have to do it. But if they think it works for them to do it, it helps the employer. If the employer has invested lots of training and then he or she loses their employee because they desire to stay home with their child, then they got to train somebody else. Lastly, it would help the family. If somebody remains attached to the workforce, Their income typically goes up a couple thousand dollars a year. And if someone is able to stay at home with his or her child, then typically that child does better. So we think it works all the way around. We're pushing it. Ivanka Trump is very much into it. And she's advocating on behalf of her father. Her father mentioned this. Donald Trump mentioned it in the state of the union address. So we think we have everything we need to get it through. We're just pushing it right now.",
