    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/1/81019.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 26,
    "turn_text": "Let me tell you how I approach public policy. I'm a conservative. I believe in the importance of faith. And in faith, we are to treat others as we would have them treat us. So I think, remember back to my childhood, where there was a great family with great parents for the times we struggled financially. And my parents would always buy my food jeans at the end of the school year, I never knew why, but now I do, because that's when everyone's there. That would have made my family's life better. What were those things that happened to my family that didn't have to happen, that if we could take care of them, would have made my family's life better? Now, if I can do unto others, as I would want somebody to do unto my family, then I'm doing my job. Now, all these things have the same thing. There are things that a couple, a parent, two parents would be, you know, just before they go to bed, the kids are asleep, they don't want to scare them. But when the kids are asleep, they then say to each other, did you see that bill we got? And we afford that? Maybe we can get a payment plan from the hospital. How does this happen? It's in network. What do we do about it? My daughter has diabetes. did you see how much the diabetes medicine costs? And we can go down the list. Oh, my mother, she's in the nursing home. Is she vulnerable to coronavirus? All those are the things I think about from the perspective of those parents trying to make it work for three generations in their lives, the parents themselves and their children. If I got public policy for that, if you, I'm doing my job. And I'm doing to others as I would ask them to do on debate. As regards to surprise medical billing, again, I've had a surprise medical bill. My wife, the surgeon, I'm a doctor and we've got an incredible surprise medical bill. If that happens to us with our knowledge based physician, it can happen to anybody. And so we're trying to protect families from that unwanted bill prescription drug. I mentioned insulin. You talk to a family with a diabetic and they can tell you about the surprise cause of Islam and how sometimes it's so expensive a family is wondering if they can afford and the diabetic is wondering if they should take. There should be no doubt about it. One you should take it so you should be able to afford it. So we're trying to address that as a kind of a real life issue.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
