    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "evening, my daughter looked at me and she goes, Daddy, I have a rash. I looked at it, didn't know what it was. I said, let me call my friend the dermatologist. I called my friend the dermatologist. I showed up my phone for FaceTime. My friend looked at it. He goes, I think it's this. I'll call it a prescription, pick it up, and no need to see me. A perfect example of telehealth. whereby we missed, did not have to go in for the doctor's visit, but the doctor was able to make the diagnosis. Currently, under Medicare, there's limitations on how telehealth is used. My point, if the recommendation for someone who thinks they may have coronavirus is not to come to the emergency room unless you're really sick, how do we know that that person will know when they're really sick? Wouldn't it be better if they could by TELL, FaceTime, Skype, you name it. Dial into the doctor. Doctors, this is my situation, what should I do? The doctor could speak to them over the phone, make a record entry, but hopefully send information out, send equipment out there to monitor the patient's oxygen level in their blood, their blood pressure, their pulse, and with that have a real-time sense of how the patient was doing, and that way she wouldn't need to come in unless she did, at that point, the doctor would know how to take care of her. So we're trying to expand that capability in response to this coronavirus.",
